sound-signals.body:
Sound signals are a tiny language: two durations, a number of blasts, and that is all. Once the rule of formation is clear there is nothing left to memorise.

The building blocks (art. 33, para. 2): blasts are of constant pitch; a SHORT blast lasts about one second, a PROLONGED blast about four, and the interval between successive blasts about one second. A peal of the bell also lasts about four seconds and may be replaced by strokes on a metal object (para. 3).

The vocabulary (art. 34) — to be given only when the safety of navigation and of other users requires it:
— one prolonged blast: "Attention" or "I am holding my course";
— one short blast: "I am turning to starboard";
— two short blasts: "I am turning to port";
— three short blasts: "I am going astern";
— four short blasts: "I am unable to manoeuvre";
— a series of very short blasts: "Risk of collision".
The logic is visible at a glance: a long blast announces a calm intention, and the more short blasts pile up the worse the situation — from a simple manoeuvre, to loss of control, to alarm.

What to sound them with (art. 33, para. 1): powered boats other than pleasure and sport craft use mechanically or electrically operated horns; other boats a suitable klaxon or horn; for rowing boats and sailing boats up to 15 m² of sail area a whistle is enough. Police boats on urgent service may use a two-tone alternating horn or a siren, and with the authority's agreement so may customs, fire, pollution-control and rescue boats (para. 4).
